             :intent "The agent's own `:seon.task/instructions` reach it cut. Observed (chain experiment 3, H1, default 98720b4eb): a step's 2,473-character instructions showed 1,024 characters and `…+1407` in `seon.context/current-state` and `my.task/current`; attempt 1 spent calls 10-15 recovering them with `subs` and ended :capped. The cut is the value printer's string bound `:seon.render.profile/max-string-length` (`seon.render.value/cut-node`) applied to the block's task section like any string, although AGENTS.md says an agent's own values are never clipped. Correct, at the producer: `seon.context/current-state` renders the held task's `:seon.task/instructions` (and `:seon.task/intent`) as the block's own data whole, outside the string bound (the task selector `seon.context/task-selector` already pulls them), so the value printer takes no schema-specific branch; the printer's one budget is spread over the rest of the block's shape. Proof: `seon.context-test/a-held-steps-instructions-are-shown-whole-in-its-context` (a step with 3,000 characters of instructions: the printed block holds all 3,000 and no `:seon.render.value/elision` for that key; red on HEAD), and the printed block stays within its token budget."
             :must-read #{seon.context/current-state seon.context/task-selector seon.render.value/cut-node seon.render.value/render-ai my.task/current}
